    <!-- §1 Overview -->
    <div class="dp-section" id="overview">
      <div class="dp-section-header">
        <div class="dp-section-num">§1</div>
        <h2>System Overview</h2>
      </div>
      <p>
        Regional Gateways manages the institutional connections to external settlement
        networks. The central pane is a stack of rail panels — a live <strong>Gateway
        Heartbeat</strong>, the <strong>PAPSS</strong> and <strong>RTGS</strong>
        configuration cards, a <strong>Diagnostic Hub</strong> error-code reference, and the
        <strong>Rail Bridge Logs</strong> audit trail. The right pane (Gateway Properties)
        explains each rail and the guardrails that protect config writes.
      </p>

      <figure style="margin: 1.25rem 0 1.75rem; text-align: center;">
        <img src="/docs/regional-gateways-tab.png"
             alt="bearerCORE Regional Gateways tab — Gateway Heartbeat, PAPSS and RTGS configuration cards, error-code diagnostic hub, and Rail Bridge Logs"
             style="width: 100%; max-width: 1180px; height: auto; border: 1px solid #d7dce3; border-radius: 8px; box-shadow: 0 2px 10px rgba(16,24,40,.08);"
             loading="lazy">
        <figcaption style="margin-top: .5rem; font-size: 12px; color: #6c757d;">
          The Regional Gateways tab in the bearerCORE desktop client — Gateway Heartbeat,
          PAPSS &amp; RTGS configuration, the Diagnostic Hub, and Rail Bridge Logs.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>

      <h3>The Four Rails</h3>
      <div class="dp-table-wrap"><table class="dp-table">
        <thead><tr><th>Rail</th><th>Protocol</th><th>Role</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td><strong>PAPSS Clearing Rail</strong></td><td><code>ISO 20022 / pacs.008</code></td><td>Pan-African Payment and Settlement System — credit-transfer clearing across AU corridors.</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>RTGS Reserve Interface</strong></td><td><code>ISO 20022 / pacs.009</code></td><td>National real-time gross settlement — verifies commercial-bank reserve before a CBDC mint.</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>DLT Corridor Node</strong></td><td><code>JSON-RPC 2.0</code></td><td>Distributed-ledger peer for corridor state and anchoring.</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>ISO 20022 Message Bus</strong></td><td><code>camt.056</code></td><td>Message transport / acknowledgement bus for cancellation &amp; status flows.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table></div>

      <div class="dp-callout dp-info"><span class="dp-ci">ℹ</span><div>The <strong>RTGS Reserve Interface</strong> here is the operator-facing configuration and monitoring surface for the national RTGS rail. It is distinct from the production <code>POST /api/v2/rtgs/inbound/pacs009</code> ingest gateway documented in <a href="/docs/issuance#rtgs">Issuance §11</a> — see <a href="#pipeline">§8</a> for how the two relate.</div></div>
    </div>

    <!-- §2 Gateway Heartbeat -->
    <div class="dp-section" id="heartbeat">
      <div class="dp-section-header">
        <div class="dp-section-num">§2</div>
        <h2>Gateway Heartbeat — Live Connectivity</h2>
      </div>
      <p>A connectivity strip that auto-refreshes every <strong>60 seconds</strong> and on demand via <strong>Ping All Gateways</strong>. Each row reports the rail, its protocol, a status LED, and the timestamp of the last handshake. The RTGS row can also be driven by a <strong>live probe</strong> — see <a href="#rtgs">§4 · Verify RTGS Reserve</a>.</p>
      <div class="dp-table-wrap"><table class="dp-table">
        <thead><tr><th>Status LED</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>🟢 <strong>ONLINE / CONNECTED</strong></td><td>Rail reachable. <em>ONLINE</em> from Ping All Gateways; <em>CONNECTED</em> is the live result of Verify RTGS Reserve (a real <code>GET /api/v2/rtgs/status</code>), reported with the last settlement time and today's count.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>🟠 <strong>IDLE</strong></td><td>RTGS reserve interface reachable, but no settlement received recently (a quiet ledger) — healthy, not an error.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>⚪ <strong>UNKNOWN / NOT CONFIGURED</strong></td><td>No probe yet this session, or the endpoint is unconfigured — neutral.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>🔴 <strong>CONFIG ERROR / OFFLINE</strong></td><td>Endpoint configured but the check failed; the matching error code is written to Rail Bridge Logs.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table></div>
      <div class="dp-rule">A red LED is always paired with a diagnostic code in the Rail Bridge Logs — match it against the <a href="#errors">Diagnostic Hub</a> to decide whether the failure is technical or compliance.</div>
      <div class="dp-callout dp-info"><span class="dp-ci">ℹ</span><div><strong>Ping All Gateways</strong> reports each rail's configuration state; <strong>Verify RTGS Reserve</strong> performs a genuine HTTP liveness probe of the RTGS rail's <code>/api/v2/rtgs/status</code> endpoint. An unconfigured rail reads UNKNOWN (grey), not red — the status only turns red once an endpoint is set but a check or required field (e.g. a routing BIC) fails.</div></div>
    </div>

    <!-- §3 PAPSS -->
    <div class="dp-section" id="papss">
      <div class="dp-section-header">
        <div class="dp-section-num">§3</div>
        <h2>PAPSS — Pan-African Payment and Settlement System</h2>
      </div>
      <p>Configuration card for the regional clearing rail (ISO 20022 / pacs.008). Unset fields show a grey placeholder hint rather than a stored value.</p>
      <div class="dp-table-wrap"><table class="dp-table">
        <thead><tr><th>Field</th><th>Purpose</th><th>Placeholder / Example</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Gateway Type</td><td>Rail template selector</td><td>PAPSS Regional Rail (ISO 20022 / pacs.008)</td></tr>
          <tr><td>API Endpoint</td><td>Clearing API base URL</td><td><code>https://api.papss.afreximbank.com/v1</code></td></tr>
          <tr><td>API Key</td><td>Bearer credential (masked)</td><td>••••</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Organisation ID</td><td>Participant identifier</td><td><code>e.g. AZA-CB-001</code></td></tr>
          <tr><td>SSL Client Cert Path</td><td>mTLS client certificate on disk</td><td><code>/etc/smartcheq/certs/papss-client.pem</code></td></tr>
          <tr><td>Cert Fingerprint (SHA-256)</td><td>Read-only — confirms remote mTLS identity</td><td>auto-populated on successful handshake</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Last Successful Sync</td><td>Read-only timestamp of last good clearing sync</td><td>2026-06-19 21:28:32</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Maintenance Mode</td><td>Pause automatic PAPSS settlements (manual reconciliation)</td><td>checkbox</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table></div>
      <h3>Actions</h3>
      <div class="dp-rule"><strong>Test PAPSS Connection</strong> — performs a handshake and writes the result to Rail Bridge Logs.</div>
      <div class="dp-rule"><strong>Save PAPSS Config</strong> — persists the card. Disabled for Forensic Auditors.</div>
      <div class="dp-rule"><strong>View PAPSS Logs</strong> — filters the Rail Bridge Logs to PAPSS entries.</div>
    </div>

    <!-- §4 RTGS -->
    <div class="dp-section" id="rtgs">
      <div class="dp-section-header">
        <div class="dp-section-num">§4</div>
        <h2>RTGS — Real-Time Gross Settlement Interface</h2>
      </div>
      <p>Configuration card for the national RTGS rail (ISO 20022 / pacs.009). The RTGS interface verifies a commercial bank's reserve balance before a CBDC mint.</p>
      <div class="dp-table-wrap"><table class="dp-table">
        <thead><tr><th>Field</th><th>Purpose</th><th>Placeholder / Example</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Gateway Type</td><td>Rail template selector</td><td>RTGS National Rail (ISO 20022 / pacs.009)</td></tr>
          <tr><td>RTGS Endpoint</td><td>RTGS API base URL</td><td><code>https://rtgs-sandbox.centralbank.internal/api</code></td></tr>
          <tr><td>Routing Code (BIC)</td><td>Settlement routing identifier</td><td><code>e.g. AZANCBXX</code></td></tr>
          <tr><td>Reserve Account ID</td><td>Central-bank reserve account to query</td><td><code>e.g. RES-AZ-RESERVE-01</code></td></tr>
          <tr><td>Query Timeout (ms)</td><td>Reserve-check timeout</td><td><code>5000</code></td></tr>
          <tr><td>Cert Fingerprint (SHA-256)</td><td>Read-only — confirms remote mTLS identity</td><td>auto-populated on successful handshake</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Last Reserve Verify</td><td>Read-only timestamp of last reserve verification</td><td>2026-06-22 09:45:12</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Maintenance Mode</td><td>Pause automatic RTGS reserve checks (manual reconciliation)</td><td>checkbox</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table></div>
      <h3>Actions</h3>
      <div class="dp-rule"><strong>Verify RTGS Reserve</strong> — performs a live <code>GET {host}/api/v2/rtgs/status</code> probe, where the host is derived from the configured RTGS Endpoint. The heartbeat RTGS row goes <strong>CHECKING…</strong> → <strong>🟢 CONNECTED</strong> (reachable, with last settlement time + today's count), <strong>🟠 IDLE</strong> (reachable but no recent settlement), or <strong>🔴 OFFLINE</strong> (network error). Last Reserve Verify and every outcome are written to Rail Bridge Logs.</div>
      <div class="dp-rule"><strong>Save RTGS Config</strong> — persists the card to <code>scconfig.xml</code>, and validates that a non-empty endpoint begins with <code>http://</code> or <code>https://</code> (otherwise rejected and not saved). Disabled for Forensic Auditors.</div>
      <div class="dp-rule"><strong>View RTGS Logs</strong> — filters the Rail Bridge Logs to RTGS entries.</div>
      <div class="dp-callout dp-info"><span class="dp-ci">ℹ</span><div><strong>Endpoints are config-driven, never compiled in.</strong> Values are read from / written to the local secure config (<code>scconfig.xml</code>), so the same build runs across dev, sandbox, and production by configuration alone. The <strong>Gateway Type</strong> selector only suggests a placeholder hint — it never overwrites a configured or typed endpoint.</div></div>
      <div class="dp-callout dp-warn"><span class="dp-ci">⚠</span><div>A populated <strong>RTGS Endpoint</strong> with an empty <strong>Routing Code (BIC)</strong> raises <code>[BE05] Gateway Address Error</code> and flips the heartbeat LED red until the BIC is supplied.</div></div>
    </div>

    <!-- §5 Error Codes -->
    <div class="dp-section" id="errors">
      <div class="dp-section-header">
        <div class="dp-section-num">§5</div>
        <h2>PAPSS / RTGS Error Code Reference — Diagnostic Hub</h2>
      </div>
      <p>When the heartbeat LED turns red, the error code appears in Rail Bridge Logs. Match it here to decide whether the failure is technical or compliance.</p>
      <div class="dp-table-wrap"><table class="dp-table">
        <thead><tr><th>Code</th><th>bearerCORE Alert</th><th>Operator Instruction</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td><code>RJ01</code></td><td>⚠ Reserves Exhausted</td><td>Verify RTGS liquidity before re-attempting.</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>BE05</code></td><td>⏱ Gateway Address Error</td><td>Verify the target BIC in Regional Gateways config.</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>AG01</code></td><td>⛔ Compliance Block</td><td>Flagged by PAPSS; check AML / Sanctions list.</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>TM01</code></td><td>⏱ Network Latency Alert</td><td>Transient — retry; escalate if persistent.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table></div>
    </div>

    <!-- §6 Rail Bridge Logs -->
    <div class="dp-section" id="logs">
      <div class="dp-section-header">
        <div class="dp-section-num">§6</div>
        <h2>Rail Bridge Logs — External Handshake Audit Trail</h2>
      </div>
      <p>A live console of every external handshake — pings, test connections, reserve verifications, and the resulting acknowledgements or error codes. A filter bar scopes the view to <strong>All</strong>, <strong>PAPSS</strong>, <strong>RTGS</strong>, or <strong>DLT</strong>.</p>
      <div class="dp-rule">Lines carrying a known error code are enriched with a severity glyph, e.g. <code>⚠ [BE05] GATEWAY ADDRESS ERROR | …</code>, so operators triage at a glance.</div>
      <div class="dp-callout dp-danger"><span class="dp-ci">⛔</span><div><strong>Clear Logs</strong> wipes the live visibility window into ongoing rail negotiations and is irreversible — the action is guarded by an explicit confirmation prompt before the audit buffer is cleared.</div></div>
    </div>

    <!-- §7 Access & Guardrails -->
    <div class="dp-section" id="access">
      <div class="dp-section-header">
        <div class="dp-section-num">§7</div>
        <h2>Access Control &amp; Guardrails</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="dp-rule"><strong>Restricted</strong> to System Administrators and Forensic Auditors.</div>
      <div class="dp-rule"><strong>Forensic Auditors — read-only.</strong> All Save / edit actions and config fields are disabled; the view is for inspection only.</div>
      <div class="dp-rule"><strong>Maintenance Mode</strong> pauses auto-settlement on the affected rail and raises an alert to Issuance and Corridor Ops.</div>
      <div class="dp-rule"><strong>Cert Fingerprint (SHA-256)</strong> is read-only and confirms the mTLS identity of the remote gateway endpoint; it is populated only on a successful handshake.</div>
      <div class="dp-callout dp-warn"><span class="dp-ci">⚠</span><div>Endpoint URL changes require <strong>dual-authorisation confirmation</strong> before they are written to config — a single operator cannot silently re-point a settlement rail.</div></div>
    </div>

    <!-- §8 End-to-End Flow -->
    <div class="dp-section" id="flow">
      <div class="dp-section-header">
        <div class="dp-section-num">§8</div>
        <h2>End-to-End Flow</h2>
      </div>
      <p>Bringing a settlement rail online and into live operation — illustrated with the RTGS reserve interface (the PAPSS rail follows the same shape with its own fields).</p>
      <div class="dp-callout dp-info"><span class="dp-ci">ℹ</span><div><strong>An unconfigured rail is UNKNOWN, not failed.</strong> A grey heartbeat LED is the correct cold-start; the rail only turns red once an endpoint is set but a handshake or required field is missing.</div></div>
      <div class="dp-flow">
        <div class="dp-step"><h4>1 · Configure endpoint &amp; identity</h4><p>Set the RTGS Endpoint, Routing Code (BIC), and Reserve Account ID, and point SSL Client Cert Path at the mTLS client certificate. A change to the endpoint URL triggers the dual-authorisation confirmation before it is written to config.</p></div>
        <div class="dp-step"><h4>2 · Probe liveness</h4><p>Click <strong>Verify RTGS Reserve</strong>. bearerCORE issues a real <code>GET /api/v2/rtgs/status</code> against the host derived from the configured endpoint; the RTGS heartbeat row shows <strong>CHECKING…</strong> while the request is in flight, and the result is appended to Rail Bridge Logs. (<strong>Ping All Gateways</strong> separately reports each rail's configuration state.)</p></div>
        <div class="dp-step"><h4>3 · Heartbeat resolves</h4><p>The row resolves from the actual reply: <strong>🟢 CONNECTED</strong> (reachable, with last settlement time + today's count), <strong>🟠 IDLE</strong> (reachable but no recent settlement — a quiet ledger), or <strong>🔴 OFFLINE</strong> (network error). Last Reserve Verify is stamped on every successful reply.</p></div>
        <div class="dp-step"><h4>4 · Diagnose &amp; remediate</h4><p>If the row is red, match the code in the Diagnostic Hub (§5) — <code>RJ01</code> reserves, <code>BE05</code> address/BIC, <code>AG01</code> compliance, <code>TM01</code> latency — fix the offending field and re-verify until it reports CONNECTED (or IDLE on a quiet ledger).</p></div>
        <div class="dp-step"><h4>5 · Settlements flow</h4><p>With the rail live, the reserve interface verifies a commercial bank's balance before each CBDC mint, and inbound <code>pacs.009</code> settlements are accepted by the issuance pipeline (§9). Reserve verifications and acknowledgements stream into Rail Bridge Logs.</p></div>
        <div class="dp-step"><h4>6 · Monitor &amp; pause</h4><p>Ongoing: the 60-second heartbeat and the handshake audit trail give continuous visibility. Toggling <strong>Maintenance Mode</strong> pauses automatic settlement on the rail and alerts Issuance and Corridor Ops for manual reconciliation.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="dp-callout dp-ok"><span class="dp-ci">✓</span><div>End state: the rail reports 🟢 CONNECTED (or 🟠 IDLE on a quiet ledger) from a live <code>/api/v2/rtgs/status</code> probe, Last Reserve Verify is current, and every negotiation is recorded in the external handshake audit trail — the rail is cleared for live settlement.</div></div>
    </div>

    <!-- §9 Integration with the RTGS Issuance Pipeline -->
    <div class="dp-section" id="pipeline">
      <div class="dp-section-header">
        <div class="dp-section-num">§9</div>
        <h2>Integration with the RTGS Issuance Pipeline</h2>
      </div>
      <p>
        Regional Gateways configures and monitors the rails; the actual settlement-to-mint
        flow runs through the inbound RTGS gateway documented in
        <a href="/docs/issuance#rtgs">Issuance §11</a>. The two surfaces share one reserve
        interface but play different roles.
      </p>
      <div class="dp-table-wrap"><table class="dp-table">
        <thead><tr><th></th><th>Regional Gateways (this page)</th><th>Inbound RTGS Gateway (Issuance §11)</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td><strong>Role</strong></td><td>Configure &amp; monitor the rail</td><td>Ingest live pacs.009 settlements</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Direction</strong></td><td>Operator → config / health checks</td><td>Commercial bank → <code>POST /api/v2/rtgs/inbound/pacs009</code></td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Output</strong></td><td>Endpoint config, heartbeat, audit log</td><td>Pre-cleared d-CBDC trade claim → SELECT SOURCE</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Security</strong></td><td>mTLS fingerprint, dual-auth config writes</td><td>mTLS + shared-secret webhook, idempotent ingest</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table></div>
      <div class="dp-callout dp-ok"><span class="dp-ci">✓</span><div>The Corridor Ops gateway strip also surfaces an <strong>● RTGS Reserve: CONNECTED / IDLE / UNKNOWN</strong> heartbeat from <code>GET /api/v2/rtgs/status</code> — the same reserve interface, reflected where corridor operators work.</div></div>
    </div>

  </div><!-- /dp-main -->

